Audio data converts samples to and from 64-bit floats

AudioData's sample formats are integers of 8 to 32 bits and 32-bit floats, and a conversion between two of them goes through single precision or through a 32-bit integer: a Pointer into an integer format copies from another format with setAsInt32 (source.getAsInt32()), and one into Float32 with setAsFloat (source.getAsFloat()). There is no 64-bit format, and no way to read or write a sample as a double. So a device that converts between its own sample format and a callback's buffers with AudioData, as WASAPI and ALSA do, has nothing wider than a float to hand a callback, and nothing to convert a double with.

Float64 is a format of 64-bit floats beside Float32, in either byte order, and every format can be read and written as a double: Pointer has getAsDouble() and setAsDouble(), and the formats have getAsDoubleLE/BE() and setAsDoubleLE/BE(). An integer format reads as its integer over the power of two it is scaled by -- 2^7, 2^15, 2^23 or 2^31 -- which is exact. It is written from a double scaled by the same power of two, rounded once to the nearest integer and limited to the range its setAsFloat() keeps, so that a sample read and written again keeps its value -- all but the most negative value of a signed format, which setAsFloat() never writes either. Int24in32 is read from its low three bytes, as getAsInt32() reads it, since a device may leave the fourth byte zero, and is written as the signed number it is. Float32 reads and writes a double with the one rounding of the conversion between the two types.

The integer formats' copyFromLE/BE() write through setAsDouble() when the source is a Float64 pointer, which Pointer::isDoublePrecision() tells them at compile time, and through the 32-bit integer as before otherwise, and Float64 copies from any format with getAsDouble(). So a conversion from doubles rounds once, to the width it writes; a conversion into doubles is exact; and every conversion between the formats that were there before is the one it was.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

